Here's a C++ solution to the problem. The idea is to check if the string is already "abc" or if by swapping any two characters once, we can make it "abc".

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

bool canBecomeABC(string s) {
    // If already "abc"
    if (s == "abc") return true;

    // Try swapping any two characters once
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
        for (int j = i + 1; j < 3; j++) {
            swap(s[i], s[j]);
            if (s == "abc") return true;
            swap(s[i], s[j]); // revert back
        }
    }
    return false;
}

int main() {
    int t;
    cin >> t;
    while (t--) {
        string s;
        cin >> s;
        if (canBecomeABC(s))
            cout << "YES\n";
        else
            cout << "NO\n";
    }
    return 0;
}
```

### Explanation:
- First, check if the string is already "abc".
- If not, try all possible swaps of two characters (there are 3 cards, so at most 3 swaps).
- If any swapped version equals "abc", print "YES".
- Otherwise, print "NO".

This satisfies the requirement of at most one swap.

To add a **Toolbar** in a **Fragment** in Android, follow these general steps:

---

### 1. Add Toolbar to Fragment Layout XML

First, define a `Toolbar` in your fragment's layout XML file (`fragment_example.xml`):

```xml
<LinearLayout xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:app="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res-auto"
    android:orientation="vertical"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent">

    <androidx.appcompat.widget.Toolbar
        android:id="@+id/toolbar"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="?attr/actionBarSize"
        android:background="?attr/colorPrimary"
        app:title="Fragment Toolbar"
        app:titleTextColor="@android:color/white" />

    <!-- Other UI elements -->

</LinearLayout>
```

---

### 2. Set Up the Toolbar in Fragment Code

In your Fragment class (e.g., `ExampleFragment`), set up the Toolbar as the support ActionBar.

```java
public class ExampleFragment extends Fragment {

    private Toolbar toolbar;

    @Nullable
    @Override
    public View onCreateView(@NonNull LayoutInflater inflater, 
                             @Nullable ViewGroup container,
                             @Nullable Bundle savedInstanceState) {
        View view = inflater.inflate(R.layout.fragment_example, container, false);

        toolbar = view.findViewById(R.id.toolbar);

        // Important: To use Toolbar as ActionBar, the hosting Activity must be AppCompatActivity
        AppCompatActivity activity = (AppCompatActivity) getActivity();
        if (activity != null) {
            activity.setSupportActionBar(toolbar);

            // Optional: set title or navigation icon
            // activity.getSupportActionBar().setTitle("My Fragment Toolbar");
            // activity.getSupportActionBar().setDisplayHomeAsUpEnabled(true);
        }

        setHasOptionsMenu(true); // If fragment has menu items

        return view;
    }

    // Override onCreateOptionsMenu if you have menu items in the toolbar
    @Override
    public void onCreateOptionsMenu(@NonNull Menu menu, @NonNull MenuInflater inflater) {
        inflater.inflate(R.menu.fragment_menu, menu);
        super.onCreateOptionsMenu(menu, inflater);
    }

    @Override
    public boolean onOptionsItemSelected(@NonNull MenuItem item) {
        if (item.getItemId() == android.R.id.home) {
            // Handle navigation icon press (back arrow)
            getActivity().onBackPressed();
            return true;
        }
        // Handle other menu item clicks
        return super.onOptionsItemSelected(item);
    }
}
```

---

### Important Notes:

- **Hosting Activity** must extend `AppCompatActivity` since we use `setSupportActionBar`.
- If your Activity already has a Toolbar set as ActionBar, this will replace it while the fragment is active.
- Alternatively, you can **keep the Toolbar in the Activity** and just update it from the Fragment.
- Use `setHasOptionsMenu(true)` in the Fragment to allow toolbar menu items.
- To add navigation (back button), call `getSupportActionBar().setDisplayHomeAsUpEnabled(true)`.

---

### Summary

- Add a Toolbar widget in your fragment layout.
- In fragment’s `onCreateView`, find the Toolbar and set it as support ActionBar via the hosting AppCompatActivity.
- Handle menus and navigation as needed.

---

