#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int main() {
    int T;
    scanf("%d", &T);
    while (T--) {
        int N;
        char S[100005];
        scanf("%d %s", &N, S);

        int aliceCount = 0, bobCount = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
            if (S[i] == '1') aliceCount++;
            else bobCount++;
        }

        // The distribution is fair if and only if the difference between
        // the count of toys given to Alice and Bob is at most 1
        // Explanation:
        // Because values are non-negative and sorted ascending,
        // the difference of sums is bounded by max toy value only if
        // the difference in number of toys is at most 1.
        if (aliceCount == bobCount || aliceCount == bobCount + 1 || bobCount == aliceCount + 1)
            printf("YES\n");
        else
            printf("NO\n");
    }
    return 0;
}
```

### Explanation:
- Count the number of toys Alice gets (`aliceCount`) and Bob gets (`bobCount`).
- The distribution is fair if the difference in counts is at most 1. If the difference is larger than 1, then it's possible to assign values to toys such that the difference in total values exceeds the maximum toy value.

This logic follows from the problem's constraints and analysis of the fairness condition.

