Certainly! If you want to extract the visible text content from an HTML document using JavaScript, you can create a function that retrieves the text from the document's body, excluding the HTML tags.

Here is a simple function that returns the visible text from the entire document:

```javascript
function extractTextFromDocument() {
  // Get the body element's inner text which contains the visible text
  return document.body.innerText || "";
}
```

### Usage:
```javascript
const text = extractTextFromDocument();
console.log(text);
```

### Explanation:
- `document.body.innerText` returns the visible text content of the body, excluding all HTML tags.
- It preserves line breaks and spacing similar to how the text appears on the page.
- If you want all text including hidden elements, you could use `textContent` instead, but usually `innerText` is more appropriate for visible text extraction.

If you want to extract text from a specific part of the document or a specific element, you can modify the function to accept an element parameter:

```javascript
function extractTextFromElement(element = document.body) {
  return element.innerText || "";
}

// Example usage:
const someElement = document.querySelector("#myElement");
const text = extractTextFromElement(someElement);
console.log(text);
```

