Certainly! Here's a Java snippet using streams to achieve your requirement:

```java
import java.util.List;

public class EntryChecker {

    public static <T> T checkList(List<T> list) {
        return list.stream()
                .limit(2)  // We only need to check up to two elements
                .reduce((a, b) -> {
                    throw new IllegalStateException("More than one entry found");
                })
                .orElse(null);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> emptyList = List.of();
        List<String> oneEntry = List.of("single");
        List<String> twoEntries = List.of("first", "second");

        System.out.println(checkList(emptyList));    // prints: null
        System.out.println(checkList(oneEntry));     // prints: single

        try {
            System.out.println(checkList(twoEntries)); // throws exception
        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {
            System.err.println(e.getMessage());        // prints: More than one entry found
        }
    }
}
```

### Explanation:
- `limit(2)` ensures the stream stops after two elements, so we don't process more than necessary.
- `.reduce((a, b) -> { throw ... })`:
  - If there's exactly one element, reduce returns it.
  - If there are two or more elements, the lambda is called with the first two elements, and we throw an exception.
- `.orElse(null)` returns `null` if the list is empty.

