To prevent XPath Injection, you should avoid directly inserting user input into the XPath expression. Instead, use an XPath API that supports variable binding or properly escape the input values. Unfortunately, the standard Java XPath API (`javax.xml.xpath`) does not support variable binding directly.

A common approach to mitigate XPath injection in Java is to **sanitize the user inputs** by escaping special characters that could break the XPath query.

Here is a safe way to construct the XPath expression by escaping the inputs:

```java
import javax.xml.xpath.*;
import org.w3c.dom.*;
import javax.xml.parsers.*;
import java.io.*;
import org.xml.sax.SAXException;

public class XPathSafeExample {

    // Escape single quotes in the input for use inside XPath string literals
    private static String escapeForXPath(String input) {
        if (input.contains("'")) {
            // If input contains single quote, use concat() in XPath to build the string
            String[] parts = input.split("'");
            StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("concat(");
            for (int i = 0; i < parts.length; i++) {
                sb.append("'").append(parts[i]).append("'");
                if (i != parts.length - 1) {
                    sb.append(", \"'\", ");
                }
            }
            sb.append(")");
            return sb.toString();
        } else {
            // No single quotes, safe to enclose in single quotes
            return "'" + input + "'";
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws ParserConfigurationException, SAXException, IOException, XPathExpressionException {
        String user = "someUser";  // user input
        String pass = "somePass";  // user input

        // Escape user inputs
        String safeUser = escapeForXPath(user);
        String safePass = escapeForXPath(pass);

        String expression = "/users/user[@name=" + safeUser + " and @pass=" + safePass + "]";

        // Example XML parsing and XPath evaluation
        DocumentBuilderFactory factory = DocumentBuilderFactory.newInstance();
        DocumentBuilder builder = factory.newDocumentBuilder();
        Document doc = builder.parse(new File("users.xml"));

        XPathFactory xPathfactory = XPathFactory.newInstance();
        XPath xpath = xPathfactory.newXPath();
        XPathExpression expr = xpath.compile(expression);

        NodeList nl = (NodeList) expr.evaluate(doc, XPathConstants.NODESET);

        System.out.println("Matching users count: " + nl.getLength());
    }
}
```

### Explanation

- The `escapeForXPath` method handles input strings that may contain single quotes (`'`), which normally break XPath string literals.
- If the input contains a single quote, it builds an XPath `concat()` function call to safely include the single quotes.
- If there are no single quotes, it just wraps the input in single quotes.
- This approach prevents malicious input from breaking out of string literals and injecting XPath commands.

### Why can't we just use parameterized queries?

Unlike SQL, XPath in Java does not support parameterized queries out-of-the-box. Escaping inputs carefully is the common mitigation technique.

