Certainly! Here’s a recursive JavaScript function that retries a request up to 3 times before resolving or rejecting the promise. It uses plain Promises and does not use `await`:

```javascript
function retryRequest(requestFn, retries = 3) {
  return requestFn()
    .then(response => {
      // If request succeeds, resolve with the response
      return response;
    })
    .catch(error => {
      if (retries > 0) {
        // Retry by calling the function recursively with one less retry
        return retryRequest(requestFn, retries - 1);
      } else {
        // No retries left, reject the promise
        return Promise.reject(error);
      }
    });
}
```

### Usage example:

