If you want to download the content of a webpage directly from the browser console using JavaScript, you can fetch the page content and then trigger a download of that content as a file.

Here's a simple example you can run in the browser console:

```js
const url = 'https://example.com'; // change this to the URL you want to download

fetch(url)
  .then(response => response.text())
  .then(data => {
    const blob = new Blob([data], { type: 'text/html' });
    const a = document.createElement('a');
    a.href = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
    a.download = 'page.html'; // default filename
    document.body.appendChild(a);
    a.click();
    a.remove();
    URL.revokeObjectURL(a.href);
  })
  .catch(err => console.error('Download failed:', err));
```

### How it works:
1. `fetch(url)` fetches the page content as text.
2. Creates a Blob from the text with MIME type `text/html`.
3. Creates an `<a>` element with a URL pointing to the Blob.
4. Sets the `download` attribute to suggest a filename.
5. Programmatically clicks the link to start the download.
6. Cleans up the created elements and object URLs.

---

### Important notes:
- This works only if CORS policy allows fetching the URL from your domain. If the target URL blocks cross-origin requests, this will fail.
- For pages on the same origin, this will work without CORS issues.
- If you want to download the current page you're on, you can use `window.location.href` as the URL.

---

### Example to download the current page:

```js
fetch(window.location.href)
  .then(response => response.text())
  .then(data => {
    const blob = new Blob([data], { type: 'text/html' });
    const a = document.createElement('a');
    a.href = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
    a.download = 'current-page.html';
    document.body.appendChild(a);
    a.click();
    a.remove();
    URL.revokeObjectURL(a.href);
  })
  .catch(err => console.error('Download failed:', err));
```

