Certainly! Here's a simple example of how you can create a Sierpinski Triangle in XAML using `Path` elements and a recursive approach in code-behind to generate the points.

Since pure XAML does not support recursion or loops, the typical approach is to generate the points in the code-behind and then bind them to the UI.

Below is a WPF example where the Sierpinski Triangle is generated programmatically and displayed in a `Canvas`.

---

### Complete Example: Sierpinski Triangle in WPF (XAML + C#)

**MainWindow.xaml**

```xml
<Window x:Class="SierpinskiTriangle.MainWindow"
        xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        Title="Sierpinski Triangle" Height="600" Width="600">
    <ScrollViewer>
        <Canvas Name="DrawingCanvas" Background="White" Width="600" Height="600"/>
    </ScrollViewer>
</Window>
```

**MainWindow.xaml.cs**

```csharp
using System.Windows;
using System.Windows.Media;
using System.Windows.Shapes;

namespace SierpinskiTriangle
{
    public partial class MainWindow : Window
    {
        public MainWindow()
        {
            InitializeComponent();
            DrawSierpinskiTriangle(5); // Adjust recursion depth here
        }

        private void DrawSierpinskiTriangle(int depth)
        {
            DrawingCanvas.Children.Clear();

            // Define the main triangle points
            Point p1 = new Point(300, 20);
            Point p2 = new Point(20, 580);
            Point p3 = new Point(580, 580);

            DrawTriangle(depth, p1, p2, p3);
        }

        private void DrawTriangle(int depth, Point p1, Point p2, Point p3)
        {
            if (depth == 0)
            {
                // Draw filled triangle
                Polygon triangle = new Polygon
                {
                    Points = new PointCollection() { p1, p2, p3 },
                    Fill = Brushes.Black,
                    Stroke = Brushes.Black,
                    StrokeThickness = 1
                };
                DrawingCanvas.Children.Add(triangle);
            }
            else
            {
                // Calculate midpoints
                Point m12 = MidPoint(p1, p2);
                Point m23 = MidPoint(p2, p3);
                Point m31 = MidPoint(p3, p1);

                // Recursive calls for 3 smaller triangles
                DrawTriangle(depth - 1, p1, m12, m31);
                DrawTriangle(depth - 1, m12, p2, m23);
                DrawTriangle(depth - 1, m31, m23, p3);
            }
        }

        private Point MidPoint(Point a, Point b)
        {
            return new Point((a.X + b.X) / 2, (a.Y + b.Y) / 2);
        }
    }
}
```

---

### How it works:

- The base triangle is defined by three points: `p1`, `p2`, and `p3`.
- The `DrawTriangle` method recursively subdivides the triangle into smaller triangles by calculating midpoints between vertices.
- When the recursion depth reaches zero, it draws a filled black triangle.
- The recursion depth controls the detail level of the Sierpinski triangle.

### To run:

- Create a new WPF Application in Visual Studio.
- Replace the `MainWindow.xaml` and `MainWindow.xaml.cs` with the code above.
- Run the project.

You will see a Sierpinski triangle rendered on the window.

