Certainly! Below is an example of a C# console application that sends an HTTP POST request with a client certificate attached. It uses `HttpClient` and configures it with a `HttpClientHandler` that includes the client certificate.

Make sure you have a client certificate file (e.g., a `.pfx` or `.p12`) and its password if needed.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates;
using System.Text;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

class Program
{
    static async Task Main(string[] args)
    {
        // Path to the client certificate file (PFX or P12)
        string certPath = "path/to/your/clientcert.pfx";
        // Certificate password, if any
        string certPassword = "your_cert_password";

        // Load the client certificate
        var clientCertificate = new X509Certificate2(certPath, certPassword);

        // Create handler and add the certificate
        var handler = new HttpClientHandler();
        handler.ClientCertificates.Add(clientCertificate);

        // Optionally, accept all server certificates (useful for testing, NOT recommended for production)
        // handler.ServerCertificateCustomValidationCallback = HttpClientHandler.DangerousAcceptAnyServerCertificateValidator;

        using (var client = new HttpClient(handler))
        {
            // The URI to send the POST request to
            var uri = "https://yourserver.com/api/endpoint";

            // The content to send in the POST request
            var content = new StringContent("{\"key\":\"value\"}", Encoding.UTF8, "application/json");

            try
            {
                HttpResponseMessage response = await client.PostAsync(uri, content);

                string responseString = await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();

                Console.WriteLine($"Status: {(int)response.StatusCode} {response.ReasonPhrase}");
                Console.WriteLine("Response:");
                Console.WriteLine(responseString);
            }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
                Console.WriteLine("Error sending POST request:");
                Console.WriteLine(ex.Message);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### Notes:
- Replace `"path/to/your/clientcert.pfx"` with the actual path to your client certificate.
- Replace `"your_cert_password"` with the actual password for your certificate (or `null` if none).
- Replace the URI `"https://yourserver.com/api/endpoint"` with the actual endpoint you want to POST to.
- The example sends JSON content; you can change the content type and body as needed.
- For testing with self-signed server certificates, you can uncomment the line with `ServerCertificateCustomValidationCallback` but do **not** use this in production.

